{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_4","statement":"Breast cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the breast.","source_span":"Breast cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the breast."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_4","statement":"Breast cancer is the second most common type of cancer in American women.","source_span":"Breast cancer is the second most common type of cancer in American women."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_4","statement":"Each breast has 15 to 20 sections called lobes.","source_span":"Each breast has 15 to 20 sections called lobes"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_4","statement":"Breast cancer is second to lung cancer as a cause of cancer death in American women.","source_span":"Breast cancer is second to lung cancer as a cause of cancer death in American women."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_4","statement":"Deaths from breast cancer decreased a little bit every year between 2003 and 2012.","source_span":"deaths from breast cancer have decreased a little bit every year between 2003 and 2012"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_4","statement":"Older age is the main risk factor for most cancers.","source_span":"Older age is the main risk factor for most cancers."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_4","statement":"Women with a family history of breast cancer in a first-degree relative (mother, sister, or daughter) have an increased risk of breast cancer.","source_span":"Women with a family history of breast cancer in a first-degree relative (mother, sister, or daughter) have an increased risk of breast cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_4","statement":"Beginning menstrual periods at age 11 or younger increases the number of years breast tissue is exposed to estrogen, raising breast cancer risk.","source_span":"Early menstruation: Beginning to have menstrual periods at age 11 or younger increases the number of years the breast tissue is exposed to estrogen."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_4","statement":"Breast tissue is exposed to more estrogen in women who become pregnant for the first time after age 35 or who never become pregnant.","source_span":"breast tissue is exposed to more estrogen in women who become pregnant for the first time after age 35 or who never become pregnant"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_4","statement":"NCI's Breast Cancer Risk Assessment Tool estimates a woman's risk for breast cancer during the next five years and up to age 90, and is meant to be used by a health care provider.","source_span":"NCI's Breast Cancer Risk Assessment Tool uses a woman's risk factors to estimate her risk for breast cancer during the next five years and up to age 90. This online tool is meant to be used by a health care provider."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_4","statement":"For more information on breast cancer risk, call 1-800-4-CANCER.","source_span":"For more information on breast cancer risk, call 1-800-4-CANCER."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_4","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What is breast cancer?","answer":"Breast cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the breast."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_4","fact_refs":[1,3],"question":"Where does breast cancer rank as a cause of new cancer cases and as a cause of cancer death in American women?","answer":"Breast cancer is the second most common type of cancer in American women (after skin cancer) and is second to lung cancer as a cause of cancer death in American women."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_4","fact_refs":[2],"question":"How many lobes does each breast typically have?","answer":"Each breast typically has 15 to 20 sections called lobes."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_4","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How did breast cancer death rates change in the US between 2003 and 2012?","answer":"Deaths from breast cancer decreased a little bit every year between 2003 and 2012."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_4","fact_refs":[7,8],"question":"How do early menstruation and older age at first birth affect a woman's breast cancer risk?","answer":"Beginning menstrual periods at age 11 or younger increases the years breast tissue is exposed to estrogen, and becoming pregnant for the first time after age 35 (or never becoming pregnant) also means more estrogen exposure — both increase breast cancer risk."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_4","fact_refs":[9,10],"question":"What does NCI's Breast Cancer Risk Assessment Tool estimate, and what number can you call for more information on breast cancer risk?","answer":"The tool estimates a woman's risk of developing breast cancer during the next five years and up to age 90, and is meant for use by a health care provider; for more information, call 1-800-4-CANCER."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_5","statement":"Breast cancer is the second leading cause of death from cancer in American women.","source_span":"Breast cancer is the second leading cause of death from cancer in American women."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_5","statement":"Each breast has 15 to 20 sections called lobes.","source_span":"Each breast has 15 to 20 sections called lobes"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_5","statement":"Breast cancer occurs more often in white women than in black women, but black women are more likely than white women to die from the disease.","source_span":"Breast cancer occurs more often in white women than in black women. However, black women are more likely than white women to die from the disease."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_5","statement":"Thermography is a procedure in which a special camera that senses heat records the temperature of the skin covering the breasts to make a temperature map.","source_span":"Thermography is a procedure in which a special camera that senses heat is used to record the temperature of the skin that covers the breasts. A computer makes a map of the breast showing the changes in temperature."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_5","statement":"There have been no clinical trials of thermography to determine how well it detects breast cancer or whether it decreases the risk of dying from breast cancer.","source_span":"There have been no clinical trials of thermography to find out how well it detects breast cancer or if having the procedure decreases the risk of dying from breast cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_5","statement":"Three tissue-sampling methods being studied for breast cancer detection are fine-needle aspiration, nipple aspiration, and ductal lavage.","source_span":"Three ways of taking tissue samples are being studied: Fine-needle aspiration... Nipple aspiration... Ductal lavage"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000027_5","statement":"Ductal lavage uses a hair-size catheter inserted into the nipple, releasing a small amount of salt water into the duct to pick up breast cells.","source_span":"A hair-size catheter (tube) is inserted into the nipple and a small amount of salt water is released into the duct. The water picks up breast cells and is removed."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_5","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What rank does breast cancer hold as a cause of cancer death among American women?","answer":"Breast cancer is the second leading cause of death from cancer in American women."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_5","fact_refs":[1],"question":"How many lobes does each breast contain?","answer":"Each breast contains 15 to 20 sections called lobes."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_5","fact_refs":[2],"question":"How does breast cancer incidence and mortality differ between white and black women in the US?","answer":"Breast cancer occurs more often in white women than in black women, but black women are more likely than white women to die from the disease."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_5","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What is thermography and how is it used in relation to breast cancer detection?","answer":"Thermography is a procedure using a special heat-sensing camera to record the skin temperature over the breasts, with a computer mapping temperature changes that tumors can cause."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_5","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Has thermography been proven through clinical trials to detect breast cancer or reduce death risk?","answer":"No — there have been no clinical trials of thermography showing how well it detects breast cancer or whether it decreases the risk of dying from it."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000027_5","fact_refs":[6],"question":"How does ductal lavage collect breast cells for sampling?","answer":"Ductal lavage inserts a hair-size catheter into the nipple and releases a small amount of salt water into the duct, which picks up breast cells that are then removed."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_1","statement":"Skin cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the skin.","source_span":"Skin cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the skin."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_1","statement":"The most common types of skin cancer are basal cell carcinoma and squamous cell carcinoma, which are nonmelanoma skin cancers.","source_span":"The most common types are basal cell carcinoma and squamous cell carcinoma, which are nonmelanoma skin cancers."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_1","statement":"Nonmelanoma skin cancers rarely spread to other parts of the body, while melanoma is more likely to invade nearby tissues and spread.","source_span":"Nonmelanoma skin cancers rarely spread to other parts of the body. Melanoma is a much rarer type of skin cancer. It is more likely to invade nearby tissues and spread to other parts of the body."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_1","statement":"Actinic keratosis is a skin condition that sometimes becomes squamous cell carcinoma.","source_span":"Actinic keratosis is a skin condition that sometimes becomes squamous cell carcinoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_1","statement":"The skin's two main layers are the epidermis (upper/outer layer) and the dermis (lower/inner layer).","source_span":"the two main layers are the epidermis (upper or outer layer) and the dermis (lower or inner layer)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_1","statement":"Melanocytes are cells found in the lower part of the epidermis that make melanin, the pigment that gives skin its natural color.","source_span":"Melanocytes: Cells that make melanin and are found in the lower part of the epidermis. Melanin is the pigment that gives skin its natural color."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_1","statement":"Skin cancer is most common in skin that is often exposed to sunlight, such as the face, neck, hands, and arms.","source_span":"it is most common in skin that is often exposed to sunlight, such as the face, neck, hands, and arms"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_1","statement":"Risk factors for basal cell carcinoma and squamous cell carcinoma include long-term sunlight or tanning bed exposure, fair complexion, having actinic keratosis, past radiation treatment, a weakened immune system, certain gene changes, and arsenic exposure.","source_span":"Risk factors for basal cell carcinoma and squamous cell carcinoma include the following: Being exposed to natural sunlight or artificial sunlight (such as from tanning beds) over long periods of time... Having actinic keratosis. Past treatment with radiation. Having a weakened immune system... Being exposed to arsenic."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_1","statement":"There are four main types of skin biopsies: shave biopsy, punch biopsy, incisional biopsy, and excisional biopsy.","source_span":"There are four main types of skin biopsies: Shave biopsy... Punch biopsy... Incisional biopsy... Excisional biopsy"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_1","statement":"In Stage I nonmelanoma skin cancer the tumor is no more than 2 centimeters, while in Stage II the tumor is more than 2 centimeters wide.","source_span":"Stage I nonmelanoma skin cancer. The tumor is no more than 2 centimeters. Stage II nonmelanoma skin cancer. The tumor is more than 2 centimeters wide."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000028_1","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What is skin cancer?","answer":"Skin cancer is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the skin."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000028_1","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What are the two most common types of nonmelanoma skin cancer?","answer":"The two most common types of nonmelanoma skin cancer are basal cell carcinoma and squamous cell carcinoma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000028_1","fact_refs":[2],"question":"How does melanoma's tendency to spread compare with that of nonmelanoma skin cancers?","answer":"Nonmelanoma skin cancers rarely spread to other parts of the body, whereas melanoma is more likely to invade nearby tissues and spread elsewhere in the body."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000028_1","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What skin condition can sometimes develop into squamous cell carcinoma?","answer":"Actinic keratosis is a skin condition that sometimes becomes squamous cell carcinoma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000028_1","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What do melanocytes do and where in the skin are they located?","answer":"Melanocytes are cells found in the lower part of the epidermis that make melanin, the pigment that gives skin its natural color."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000028_1","fact_refs":[9],"question":"What tumor size distinguishes Stage I from Stage II nonmelanoma skin cancer?","answer":"In Stage I, the tumor is no more than 2 centimeters; in Stage II, the tumor is more than 2 centimeters wide."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000028_1","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What are the four main types of skin biopsy used to diagnose skin cancer?","answer":"The four main types of skin biopsy are shave biopsy, punch biopsy, incisional biopsy, and excisional biopsy."}
